===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}}
## {{senseid|en|amount of space}} An [[amount#Noun|amount]] of [[space#Noun|space]] between [[point#Noun|points]] (often [[geographical#Adjective|geographical]] points), usually (but not [[necessarily]]) [[measure#Verb|measured]] along a [[straight line]].
##: {{syn|en|spread}}
##* {{RQ:Hakluyt Principall Navigations|author=w:Arthur Barlowe|chapter=The First Voyage Made to the Coastes of America, with Two Barkes, wherein were Captaines Master {{w|Philip Amadas}}, and Master Arthur Barlowe, who Discouered Part of the Countrey, Now Called Virginia, Anno 1584. Written by One of the Said Captaines, and Sent to Sir {{w|Walter Raleigh}}, Knight, at whose Charge and Direction, the Said Voyage was Set foorth.|year=1584|page=731|passage=[S]he {{...}} gaue vs into our boate our ſupper halfe dreſſed, pots, and all, and brought vs to our boates ſide, in which wee laye all night, remoouing the ſame a pretie '''diſtance''' from the ſhoare: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|act=IV|scene=i|sig=G2|page=61|passage=The prince is here at hand, pleaſeth your Lordſhip / To meet his grace iuſt '''diſtance''' tvveene our armies.}}
##* {{RQ:T. Herbert Travaile|chapter=[Holy Port]|page=3|passage=The third of ''Aprill'', early in the morning, vvee had ſight of the ''Holy Port'' {{quote-gloss|[[Porto Santo]]}}, belonging to the ''Spaniard'', vvhich Ile at eight leagues '''diſtance''', gaue it ſelfe in this ſhape vnto vs.}}
##* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=4|passage=Novv by this time the Man vvas got a good '''diſtance''' from them; But hovvever they vvere reſolved to purſue him; vvhich they did and in little time they over-took him<!--. missing-->}}
##* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|chapter=Of Simple Modes; and First, of the Simple Modes of Space|sections=2–3|page=75|passage=[W]e get the ''Idea'' of Space, both by our Sight, and Touch; vvhich, I think, is ſo evident, that it vvould be as needleſs, to go to prove, that Men perceive by their Sight, a '''diſtance''' betvveen Bodies of different Colours, or betvveen the parts of the ſame Body; as that they ſee Colours themſelves: Nor is it leſs obvious, that they can do ſo in the Dark by Feeling and Touch. This Space conſidered barely in length betvveen any tvvo Beings, vvithout conſidering any thing elſe betvveen them, is called '''''diſtance''''': If conſidered in Length, Breadth, and Thickneſs, I think, it may be called ''Capacity'': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dampier New Voyage|chapter=VIII|page=225|passage=VVe kept at a good '''diſtance''' off ſhore, and ſavv no Land till the 14th day; but then, being in lat. 12 d. 50 m. the Volcan of ''Guatimala'' appeared in ſight.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian 1713|issue=167|page=2|column=1|passage=''Helim'' had placed tvvo of his ovvn Mules at about a Mile's '''Diſtance''' from the black Temple, on the Spot vvhich they had agreed upon for their Rendezvous.}}
##* {{RQ:Shelley Queen Mab|note=I. Page 13|page=125|passage=[O]bservations on the eclipses of Jupiter's satellites have demonstrated that light takes up no more than 8′ 7″ in passing from the sun to the earth, a '''distance''' of 95,000,000 miles.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=VIII|pages=53–54|pageref=54|passage=Then everybody once more knelt, and soon the blessing was pronounced. The choir and the clergy trooped out slowly, through the open screen, down the nave to the western door. {{...}} At a seemingly immense '''distance''' the surpliced group stopped to say the last prayer.}}
##* {{RQ:Maugham Razor's Edge|section=iv|page=355|passage=I have never been to Dallas, but I suppose that, like other American cities I know, it has a residential district within easy motoring '''distance''' of the business section and the country club where the affluent have fine houses in large gardens with a handsome view of hill or dale from the living-room windows.}}
### {{lb|en|horse racing}} ''Chiefly in'' '''by a distance''': a space of more than 30 [[length#Noun|lengths]] (about 80 [[yard#Noun|yards]] or 7.3 [[metre#Noun|metres]]) between [[two#Numeral|two]] [[racehorse]]s [[finish#Verb|finishing]] a [[race#Noun|race]], [[use#Verb|used]] to [[describe]] the [[margin of victory]]; also {{lb|en|archaic}}, any space of 240 yards (about 219.5 [[metre#Noun|metres]]) on a [[racecourse]].
## ''Chiefly in'' '''from a distance''': a [[place#Noun|place]] which is [[far away#Adjective|far away]] or [[remote#Adjective|remote]]; specifically {{lb|en|especially|painting}}, a more remote [[part#Noun|part]] of a [[landscape#Noun|landscape]] or [[view#Noun|view]] as [[contrast#Verb|contrasted]] with the [[foreground#Noun|foreground]].
##* {{RQ:Berkeley Vision|section=LXXII|sectionname=Objection Answer'd|page=79|passage=VVhen from a '''Diſtance''' (I ſpeak vvith the Vulgar) vve behold great Objects, the Particles of the intermediate Air and Vapours, vvhich are themſelves unperceivable, do interrupt the Rays of Light, and thereby render the Appearance leſs Strong and Vivid; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|poem=The Progress of Error|page=51|passage=Vievv'd from a '''diſtance''', and vvith heedleſs eyes, / Folly and innocence are ſo alike, / The diff'rence, though eſſential, fails to ſtrike.}}
##* {{RQ:Irving Sketch Book|title=Rip Van Winkle|page=71|passage=As he was about to descend, he heard a voice from a '''distance''', hallooing, "Rip Van Winkle! Rip Van Winkle!"}}
##* {{RQ:Darwin et al Voyages|volume=III|chapter=I|page=8|passage=The rocks of St. Paul appear from a '''distance''' of a brilliantly white colour. This is partly owing to the dung of a vast multitude of seafowl, and partly to a coating of a glossy white substance, which is intimately united to the surface of the rocks.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=104–105|pageref=105|passage=And every man on board {{quote-gloss|the ship}}, waking or sleeping, good or bad, had had a kinder word for another on that day than on any day in the year; and had shared to some extent in its festivities; and had remembered those he cared for at a '''distance''', and had known that they delighted to remember him.}}
##* {{RQ:Kingsley Hereward the Wake|volume=I|page=18|passage=Dark and sad were those short autumn days, when all the '''distances''' were shut off, and the air choked with foul brown fog and drenching rains from off the eastern sea; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Julie Gold|artist=w:Nanci Griffith|title=w:From a Distance|album=w:Lone Star State of Mind|location=Universal City, Calif.|publisher=w:MCA Records|year='''1985''' (date written)|year_published=1987|oclc=15279458|passage=From a '''distance''', you look like my friend / Even though we are at war / From a '''distance''', I just cannot comprehend / What all this fighting's for // {{...}} // God is watching us / From a '''distance'''}}
## ''Chiefly with a [[modifying#Adjective|modifying]] [[word#Noun|word]]'': a [[measure#Noun|measure]] between two points or [[quantity|quantities]]; a [[difference#Noun|difference]], a [[variance]].
##: {{coi|en|angular '''distance'''&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; focal '''distance'''}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''distance''' between the lowest and next gear on my bicycle is annoying.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 Feb 82|volume=9|number=29|journal=Gay Community News|page=10|title=Connie|author=Nancy Walker|text=By the time I encountered them, Connie's friend {{...}} was quite elderly (Connie was the younger by a considerable '''distance''')}}
## An [[interval]] or length of [[time#Noun|time]] between [[event#Noun|events]].
##* {{RQ:Bentley Epistles|page=39|passage=VVe cannot tell, at this '''diſtance''' of time, vvhich Converſation vvas firſt, that vvith ''Phalaris'', or that vvith ''Leon''.}}
##* {{RQ:Prior Poems|year=1718|chapter=Postscript|sig=d|page=27|passage=I Muſt help my Preface by a Poſtſcript, to tell the Reader, that there is Ten Years '''Diſtance''' betvveen my vvriting the One and the Other; and that (vvhatever I thought then, and have ſomevvhere ſaid, that I vvould publiſh no more Poetry) He vvill find ſeveral Copies of Verſes ſcattered through this Edition, vvhich vvere not printed in the Firſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Foote Cozeners|page=64|passage=You vvill take this draught, three times a-day, at tvvo hours' '''diſtance''', firſt ſhaking it vvell.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Playfair|chapter=Preface|title=Elements of Geometry; Containing the First Six Books of Euclid, with Two Books on the Geometry of Solids.{{nb...|To which are Added, Elements of Plane and Spherical Trigonometry.}}|location=Edinburgh|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Bell & Bradfute, and [[w:George Robinson (bookseller)|G[eorge] G[eorge] & J[ohn] Robinson]],{{nb...|London.}}|year=1795|page=iii|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=9y7JI4_79RMC&pg=PR3|oclc=1325894617|passage=IT is a remarkable fact in the hiſtory of ſcience, that the oldeſt book of Elementary Geometry is ſtill conſidered as the beſt, and that the vvritings of [[w:Euclid|{{smallcaps|Euclid}}]], at the '''diſtance''' of tvvo thouſand years, continue to form the moſt approved introduction to the mathematical ſciences.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=IV|page=455|passage=But the new sovereign's mind was haunted by an apprehension not to be mentioned, even at this '''distance''' of time, without shame and indignation.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[separation]] in some way other than space or time.
##: {{ux|en|The friendship did not survive the row: they kept each other [[at a distance|at a '''distance''']].}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello Q1|act=III|scene=iii|page=41|passage=He ſhall in ſtrangeſt, ſtand no farther off, / Then in a politique '''diſtance'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Burnet History|volume=I|pages=226–227|pageref=227|passage=The buſy men in ''Scotland'', being encouraged from ''Rotterdam'', vvent about the country, to try if any men of vveight vvould ſet themſelves at the head of their deſigns for an inſurrection. The Earl of ''Caſſilis'' {{quote-gloss|''i.e.'', {{w|John Kennedy, 6th Earl of Cassilis}}}} and ''Lockhart'' {{quote-gloss|{{w|William Lockhart of Lee}}}} vvere the tvvo perſons they reſolved to try. But they did it at ſo great a '''diſtance''', that, from the propoſition made to them, there vvas no danger of miſpriſion of treaſon.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### {{synonym of|en|length|t=an [[extent#Noun|extent]] measured along the [[long#Adjective|longest]] [[dimension#Noun|dimension]] of an [[object#Noun|object]]}}.
### {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[disagreement]], a [[dispute#Noun|dispute]]; also, an [[estrangement]].
###: {{synonyms|en|quarrel}}
### {{lb|en|music}} A difference in [[pitch#Noun|pitch]] between [[sound#Noun|sounds]]; an interval.
###* {{RQ:T. More Robinson Utopia|subchapter=Of Their Iourneyenge or Trauaylynge abrode, with Dyuers Other Matters Cunnyngly Reasoned & Wittilie Discussed|sig=&#91;M.vj.&#93;|verso=1|page=201|passage=[N]other perceaueth the concordaunt and diſcordante '''diſtaunces''' of ſoundes, and tunes {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}}
## The amount of space between points (often geographical points), usually (but not necessarily) measured along a straight line.
##: {{ux|en|The '''distance''' to Petersborough is thirty miles.}}
##: {{ux|en|From Moscow, the '''distance''' is relatively short to Saint Petersburg, relatively long to Novosibirsk, but even greater to Vladivostok.}}
### {{lb|en|boxing}}
#### The [[maximum#Adjective|maximum]] amount of space between a [[boxer]] and their [[opponent#Noun|opponent]] within which the boxer can [[punch#Verb|punch]] [[effectively]].
#### ''Often in'' '''[[go the distance]]''''','' '''[[last#Verb|last]] the distance''''', or'' '''[[stay the distance]]''': the [[scheduled#Adjective|scheduled]] duration of a [[bout#Noun|bout]].
### {{lb|en|fencing}} The amount of space between a [[fencer]] and their opponent, which the fencer [[try#Verb|tries]] to [[control#Verb|control]] in order to [[gain#Verb|gain]] an [[advantage#Noun|advantage]] over the opponent.
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|act=II|scene=i|page=45|column=1|passage={{quote-gloss|''William''}} ''Page''. I haue heard the French-man hath good skill in his Rapier. / [[w:Robert Shallow|{{quote-gloss|''Robert''}} ''Shal''&#91;''low''&#93;]]. Tut ſir: I could haue told you more: In theſe times you ſtand on '''diſtance''': your Paſſes, Stoccado's, and I knovv not vvhat: {{...}}}}
### {{lb|en|horse racing}} Originally, the space measured back from the [[winning post]] which a racehorse [[run#Verb|running]] in a [[heat#Noun|heat]] must [[reach#Verb|reach]] when the [[winner]] has [[cover#Verb|covered]] the [[whole#Adjective|whole]] [[course#Noun|course]], in order to run in a [[subsequent#Adjective|subsequent]] heat; also, the point on the racecourse that space away from the winning post; now, the point on a racecourse 240 yards from the winning post.
###* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|fablenumber=CCCXXXIX|fable=A Plain Horse Wins the Prize|page=296|passage=THere vvere a Great many Brave, Sightly ''Horſes'' vvith Rich Trappings that vvere brought out One day to the Courſe, and Only ''One Plain Nag'' in the Company that made ſport for All the reſt. But vvhen they came at laſt to the Tryal, ''This'' vvas the ''Horſe'' that ran the VVhole Field out of '''Diſtance''', and VVon the Race. / The MORAL. ''Our Senſes are No Competent Judges of the Excellencies of the Mind.''}}
### {{lb|en|military}} The amount of space between [[soldier#Noun|soldiers]] or [[cavalry]] [[rider]]s [[march#Verb|marching]] or [[stand#Verb|standing]] in a [[rank#Noun|rank]]; also, the amount of space between such ranks.
### {{lb|en|sport}} The [[complete#Adjective|complete]] length of a course over which a race is run.
## ''Chiefly preceded by'' '''[[the#Article|the]]''''', especially in'' '''[[into]]''' ''or'' '''[[in the distance]]''': the place that is far away or remote.
##* {{RQ:Shelley Queen Mab|page=21|passage=There was a little light / That twinkled in the misty '''distance''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|page=48|passage=They walked along the road; Scrooge recognising every gate, and post, and tree; until a little market-town appeared in the '''distance''', with its bridge, its church, and winding river.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|page=68|passage=[W]e hear / A trumpet in the '''distance''' pealing news / Of better, and Hope, {{...}}}}
## The state of being [[separate#Verb|separated]] from something else, especially by a long [[way#Noun|way]]; the state of being far off or remote; [[farness]], [[remoteness]].
##* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Criticism|page=12|passage=Some Figures ''monſtrous'' and ''miſ-ſhap'd'' appear, / Conſider'd ''ſingly'', or beheld too ''near'', / VVhich, but ''proportion'd'' to their ''Light'', or ''Place'', / Due '''Diſtance''' ''reconciles'' to Form and Grace.}}
##* {{RQ:Addison Cato|scene=i|page=60|passage=[[w:Julius Caesar|{{quote-gloss|Julius}} ''Cæſar'']] is ſtill diſpoſed to give us Terms, / And vvaits at '''Diſtance''' 'till he hears from ''[[w:Cato the Younger|Cato]]''.}}
##* {{RQ:Campbell Pleasures of Hope|lines=5–8|page=3|passage=VVhy do thoſe cliffs of ſhadovvy tint appear / More ſvveet than all the landſcape ſmiling near?— / 'Tis '''Diſtance''' lends enchantment to the vievv, / And robes the mountain in its azure hue.}}
##* {{RQ:Shelley Posthumous Poems|poem=Letter to -|page=68|passage=Afar the Contadino's song is heard, / Rude, but made sweet by '''distance''';—{{...|nospace=1}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|page=5|passage=But what did Scrooge care? It was the very thing he liked. To edge his way along the crowded paths of life, warning all human sympathy to keep its '''distance''', was what the knowing ones call "nuts" to Scrooge.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### The [[entire#Adjective|entire]] amount of [[progress#Noun|progress]] to an [[objective#Noun|objective]].
###: {{ux|en|He had promised to perform this task, but did not [[go the distance|go the '''distance''']].}}
### The state of remoteness or separation in some way other than space or time.
###: {{ux|en|the '''distance''' between a descendant and their ancestor}}
###: {{ux|en|We’re narrowing the '''distance''' between the two versions of the bill.}}
### The state of people not being [[close#Adjective|close]], [[friendly#Adjective|friendly]], or [[intimate#Adjective|intimate]] with each other; also, the state of people who were once close, friendly, or intimate with each other [[no longer]] being so; estrangement.
###: {{sense|state of not being close}} {{synonyms|en|alienation}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|poem=A Lover's Complaint|sig=L|page=217|passage=For ſhe vvas ſought by ſpirits of ritcheſt cote, / But kept cold '''diſtance''', and did thence remoue, / To ſpend her liuing in eternall loue.}}
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VIII|lines=6–10|sig=Ee|verso=1|page=219|passage=[F]oul diſtruſt, and breach / Diſloyal on the part of Man, revolt, / And diſobedience: On the part of Heav'n / Novv alienated, '''diſtance''' and diſtaſte, / Anger and juſt rebuke, and judgement giv'n, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=The Select Circle|page=46|passage=In former days every tavern of repute kept such a room for its own select circle—a club, or society, of ''habitués'', who met every evening for a pipe and a cheerful glass. {{...}} Strangers might enter the room, but they were made to feel that they were there on sufferance; they were received with '''distance''' and suspicion.}}
### [[excessive|Excessive]] [[reserve#Noun|reserve]] or [[lack#Noun|lack]] of [[friendliness]] [[show#Verb|shown]] by a [[person#Noun|person]]; [[aloofness]], [[coldness]].
###: {{synonyms|en|standoffishness|unfriendliness|unsociableness}}
