### The rank to which an [[important]] person [[belong#Verb|belongs]].
###* {{RQ:Donne Poems|year=1639|poem=To the Countesse of Huntington|page=191|passage=[T]o your eye, / Theſe (Madame) that vvithout your '''diſtance''' lie, / Muſt either miſt, or nothing ſeeme to be, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|book=VIII|section=II|sectionname=To Mr. Thomas Bowyer of the Old Jury Merchant|subsection=33 (Of Those who Died in Prison)|page=23|passage=I am not ſatisfied in vvhat '''diſtance''' properly to place theſe perſons. Some, perchance, vvill account it too high, to rank them amongſt ''Martyrs''; and ſurely, I conceive it too lovv, to eſteem them but bare ''Confeſſours''.}}
### The [[state#Noun|state]] of disagreement or dispute between people; [[dissension]].
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=III|scene=i|page=140|column=1|passage=[[w:Macbeth (character)|''Macb''&#91;''eth''&#93;]]. Both of you knovv ''{{w|Banquo}}'' vvas your Enemie. / ''Murth''[''erer'']. True, my Lord. / ''Macb.'' So is he mine: and in ſuch bloody '''diſtance''', / That euery minute of his being, thruſts / Againſt my neer'ſt of Life: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Seditions and Troubles|page=88|passage=Generally, the Diuiding and Breaking of all Factions, and Combinations that are aduerſe to the State, and ſetting them at '''diſtance''', or at leaſt diſtruſt amongſt themſelues, is not one of the vvorſt ''Remedies''.}}
###* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=VII|date=10 September 1667|page=107|passage=He tells me, among other things, that this business of the Chancellor do breed a kind of inward '''distance''' between the King and the Duke of York, and that it cannot be avoided; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Fielding Amelia|volume=I|chapter=Mr. Booth Continues His Story.{{nb...|In this Chapter there are Some Passages that may Serve as a Kind of Touchstone, by which a Young Lady may Examine the Heart of Her Lover. I would Advise, therefore, that Every Lover be Obliged to Read It over in the Presence of His Mistress, and that She Carefully Watch His Emotions while He is Reading}}|pages=107–108|pageref=108|passage=[T]he true Reaſon vvhy I did not mention her before, vvas, that I apprehended there vvas ſome little '''Diſtance''' betvveen them, vvhich I hoped to have the Happineſs of accommodating.}}
### ''Often followed by'' '''[[to#Preposition|to]]''' ''or'' '''[[towards#Preposition|towards]]''': an [[attitude#Noun|attitude]] of remoteness or reserve which [[respect#Noun|respect]] [[require]]s; hence, [[ceremoniousness]].
###* {{RQ:Dryden Indian Emperour|page=4|passage=[T]hough you ſee / The King is kind, I hope your modeſty / VVill knovv, vvhat '''diſtance''' to the Crovvn is due.}}
###* {{RQ:Bentley Epistles|year=1699|page=287|passage=I vvill not ſift into them too minutely; for I'll obſerve the reſpect and '''diſtance''' that's due to him from his ''Scholar:'' {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Atterbury Lord Mayor|page=7|passage=''Third'' plain Reaſon of the Publick Honours done to the Magiſtrate is, that he may not only be ſecure, but had alſo in due Eſtimation and Reverence by all thoſe vvho are ſubject to him. 'Tis by Reſpect and '''Diſtance''' that Authority is upheld; and 'tis by Outvvard Marks and Enſigns of Honour that Reſpect is ſecured; eſpecially from Vulgar Minds, vvhich do not enter into the true Reaſons of Things, but are govern'd by Appearances.}}
