===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|dissh}}, {{m|enm|disch}}, from {{inh|en|ang|disċ|t=plate; bowl; dish}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*disk|t=table; dish}} (whence also {{m+|sla-pro|*dъska}}, whence {{cog|bg|дъска́}}, {{cog|pl|deska}}, {{cog|ru|доска́}}), {{cog|ru|чан}}) from {{der|en|la|discus}}.  {{doublet|en|dais|desk|disc|discus|disk|diskos}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|disch|t=dish; plate}}, {{cog|nl|dis|t=table}}, {{cog|nds-de|Disk}}, {{m|nds-de|Disch|t=table}}, {{cog|de|Tisch|t=table}}, {{cog|da,nb,nn,sv|disk|t=dish; counter}}, {{cog|is|diskur|t=dish; plate}}, {{cog|fi|tiski|t=desk, counter; dish}}.
{{col-bottom}}

Compare the identical meaning expansion (vessel for food, then also content of such a vessel, then also specific type of food): {{m+|bg|блю́до}}, {{m+|ru|блю́до}}. For the roundness aspect, compare {{m+|pl|rondel||pan, saucepan}} (< {{m+|la|rotundus}} (whence also {{m+|en|round}})), {{m+|sl|krožnik}} < {{m|sl|krog}}. Also compare typologically {{m+|sla-pro|*misъka}} << {{m+|la|mēnsa}}; {{m+|grc|πίναξ}} (several meanings).

