===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰeygʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|diggen}} (“to dig”, 13th c.), an alteration of {{m|enm|dīken}}, from {{der|en|ang|dīcian|t=to dig a ditch, mound up earth}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*dīkōn}}, which see for cognates. This verb is denominal from {{der|en|gem-pro|*dīkaz|t=pool, puddle; [[dyke]], [[ditch]]}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰeygʷ-||to stab, dig}}.

The form with ''g'' may have been influenced by {{der|en|fro||*diguer}}, a variant of {{m|fro|dikier}}, itself from the West Germanic verb above. French forms with ''g'' are attested only in the 15th c., thus 200 years later than in English. On the other hand, French has according forms also for the underlying noun (cf. {{m|fr|digue}}) and the phonetic development is more plausible in French than in English.<!--"Additionally, {{m+|enm||diggen}} may derive from an unrecorded suffixed variant, {{m+|ang|*dicgian|*dīcgian}}." >> Please either source or explain. What suffix is this?-->

