===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|es|despachar}} and/or {{bor|en|it|dispacciare}}. Further etymology is uncertain. For first element {{af|en|des-}}, see {{af|en|dis-}}. Second element probably from either {{der|en|la-vul|*pactāre||to fasten"}}, {{m|la-vul|*pactiare}} or {{der|en|la|-pedicāre||to entrap, shackle}}, from {{m|la|pedica}}, from {{m|la|pēs}}. Ultimately related to {{l|en|impeach}} through shared Latin root. The first known use in writing (in the past tense, spelled as ''dispached'') is by Bishop {{w|Cuthbert Tunstall}} in 1517. This would be unusually early for a borrowing from a Romance language other than French, but Tunstall had studied in Italy and was Commissioner to Spain, so this word may have been borrowed through diplomatic circles. The alternative spelling {{m|en|despatch}} was introduced in {{w|Samuel Johnson}}'s dictionary, probably by accident.

Compare typologically {{m|en|deliver}} (for the meaning ''to bring or transport'') (< {{m+|la|dē-}} + {{m|la|līberō}}).

