===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-|id=drive}}
From {{inh|en|enm|digesten|}}, from {{der|en|la|dīgestus|}}, past participle of {{m|la|dīgerō||carry apart}}, from {{m|la|dī-}} (for {{m|la|dis-||apart}}) + {{m|la|gerō||to carry}}, influenced by {{cog|frm|digestion|}}. Partly displaced native {{ncog|ang|meltan}} (intransitive) and {{m|ang|mieltan}} (transitive), both “to melt, to digest,” whence {{w|Modern English}} {{m|en|melt}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|dī-jĕstʹ|də-jĕstʹ}}, {{IPA|en|/daɪˈd͡ʒɛst/|/dəˈd͡ʒɛst/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-digest-verb.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-digest.oga|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛst|s=2}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To distribute or arrange methodically; to work over and classify; to reduce to portions for ready use or application.
#: {{ux|en|to '''digest''' laws}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1783|author=w:Hugh Blair|title=Lectures on Rhetoric and Belles Lettres|passage=joining them together and '''digesting''' them into order}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|act=II|scene=ii|passage=We have cause to be glad that matters are so well '''digested'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To separate (the food) in its passage through the [[alimentary canal]] into the nutritive and nonnutritive elements; to prepare, by the action of the [[digestive juice]]s, for conversion into blood; to convert into [[chyme]].
#* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of Handling, Taming, or Breaking of Horsses|page=303|passage=In the morning giue them {{quote-gloss|horses}} barley or prouender, a little at a time in diſtinct or ſeueral portions, tvvice or thrice one after another, ſo as he may chevv and eke '''diſgeſt''' it thoroughly, othervviſe if he rauen it, as he vvil do hauing much at a time, he rendreth it in his dung vvhole and not '''diſgeſted'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|understand}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To think over and arrange methodically in the mind; to reduce to a plan or method; to receive in the mind and consider carefully; to get an understanding of; to comprehend.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1566|author=w:Henry Sidney|title=letter to Philip Sidney|passage=Feelingly '''digest''' the words you speak in prayer.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|act=III|scene=i|passage=How shall this bosom multiplied '''digest''' / The senate's courtesy?}}
#* {{RQ:Book of Common Prayer|passage=Grant that we may in such wise hear them [the Scriptures], read, mark, learn, and inwardly '''digest''' them.}}
# To bear comfortably or patiently; to be reconciled to; to brook.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1834|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|title=Table Talk - Toleration-Norwegians|passage=I never can '''digest''' the loss of most of Origen's works.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|chemistry}} To expose to a gentle heat in a boiler or [[matrass]], as a preparation for chemical operations.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[undergo]] [[digestion]].
#: {{ux|en|I just ate an omelette and I'm waiting for it to '''digest'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=III|page=78|passage=I was at the Mathematical School, where the Maſter taught his Pupils after a Method ſcarce imaginable to us in ''Europe''. The Propoſition and Demonſtration were fairly written on a thin Wafer, with Ink compoſed of a Cephalick Tincture. This the Student was to ſwallow upon a faſting Stomach, and for three days following eat nothing but Bread and Water. As the Wafer '''digeſted''', the Tincture mounted to his Brain, bearing the Propoſition along with it.}}
# {{lb|en|biochemistry|transitive|of DNA molecules}} To cut with one or more [[restriction endonuclease]]s.
# {{lb|en|medicine|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[suppurate]]; to generate [[pus]], as an [[ulcer]].
#* {{RQ:Wiseman Chirurgicall Treatises|chapter=XVIII|chaptername=Of an Oedema|page=89|passage=The Lips of the Abſceſs '''digeſted''' vvell, but from vvithin it onely gleeted, and thruſt out Fat, vvhich vve daily cut off vvithout the loſs of a drop of blood, and dreſſed up the Abſceſs vvith ''mundif. ex apio'', continuing the uſe of diſcutient Fomentations and Cataplaſins.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|obsolete|transitive}} To cause to suppurate, or generate pus, as an ulcer or wound.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To ripen; to mature.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1662|author=w:Jeremy Taylor|title=The Measures and Offices of Friendship|passage=well-'''digested''' fruits}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[quieten]] or [[reduce]] (a negative feeling, such as anger or grief).

