===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|dissipacion}}, {{m|enm|dissipacioun}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|dissipātiō}}. Morphologically {{suffix|en|dissipate|ion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌdɪsɪˈpeɪʃən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=4}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-dissipation.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[dissipating]] or [[dispersing]]; a state of [[dispersion]] or [[separation]]; dispersion; [[waste]].
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=without loss or '''dissipation''' of the matter}}
#* {{RQ:Hale Mankind|passage=the famous '''dissipation''' of mankind}}
# A [[dissolute]] course of life, in which health, money, etc., are [[squandered]] in pursuit of pleasure; [[profuseness]] in immoral indulgence, as late hours, riotous living, etc.; [[dissoluteness]].
#* '''18th century''', {{w|Patrick Henry}} in a parliamentary debate
#*: to reclaim the spendthrift from his '''dissipation''' and extravagance
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Pelham|volume=II|chapter=XX|page=196|passage=I rose by candle-light, and consumed, in the intensest application, the hours which every other individual of our party wasted in enervating slumbers, from the hesternal '''dissipation''' or debauch.}}
#* {{RQ:Emily Bronte Wuthering Heights|chapter=[https://www.gutenberg.org/files/768/768-h/768-h.htm chapter VIII]|passage=He neither wept nor prayed; he cursed and defied: execrated God and man, and gave himself up to reckless '''dissipation'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr]]|chapter=4|title=Lord Stranleigh Abroad|year=1913|ol=1796924W|passage=[...] This is a surprise attack, and I’d no wish that the garrison, forewarned, should escape. I am sure, Lord Stranleigh, that he has been descanting on the distraction of the woods and the camp, or perhaps the metropolitan '''dissipation''' of Philadelphia, [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Return of Tarzan|version=Burt|pages=34–35|pageref=34|passage=An oil lamp burned upon a high, old-fashioned mantel, casting its dim rays over a dozen repulsive figures. All but one were men. The other was a woman of about thirty. Her face, marked by low passions and '''dissipation''', might once have been lovely.}}
# A [[trifle]] which [[wastes]] time or [[distracts]] attention.
#* '''1733''' May 28, letter from {{w|Alexander Pope}} to {{w|Jonathan Swift}}:
#*: Prevented from finishing them [the letters] a thousand avocations and '''dissipations'''.
# {{lb|en|physics}} A [[loss]] of [[energy]], usually as [[heat]], from a [[dynamic]] system.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 6, 1965|journal={{w|Nature (journal)|Nature}}|volume=208|issue=575|author={{w|Giuseppe Colombo|G. Colombo}}|doi=10.1038/208575a0|title=Rotational Period of the Planet Mercury|passage=They conclude{{...|from a very brief discussion that after slowing down from a higher direct angular velocity,}} the planet will have a final period of rotation between 56 and 88 days, depending on the assumed form of the '''dissipation''' function.}}

