Recorded since c. 1374 (displacing {{noncog|ang|toliesan}}) as {{inh|en|enm|dissolven}}, from {{der|en|la|dissolvo|dissolvere|t=to loosen up, break apart}} but with the sense from {{der|en|xno|dissoldre}} (variant of {{cog|fro|dissoudre}}), itself from {{m|la|dis-|t=apart}} + {{m|la|solvo|solvere|t=to loose, loosen}}. {{surf|en|dis-|solve}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈzɒlv/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-dissolve.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈzɑlv/|/dɪˈzɔlv/|a=GA}}
* {{hyph|en|dis|solve}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒlv|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[terminate]] a [[union]] of multiple members actively, as by [[disband]]ing.
#: {{ant|en|establish|found}}
#: {{ux|en|The ruling party or coalition sometimes '''dissolves''' parliament early when the polls are favorable, hoping to reconvene with a larger majority.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[destroy]], make [[disappear]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[liquify]], [[melt]] into a [[fluid]].
#: {{syn|en|melt|formelt}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|III|ii|passage=as if the world were all '''dissolved''' to tears}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[melt]]ed, changed into a [[fluid]].
# {{lb|en|physical chemistry|transitive}} To [[disintegrate]] [[chemically]] into a [[solution]] by [[immersion]] into a [[liquid]] or other material.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=Harry E. Wedeck|title=Dictionary of Aphrodisiacs|publisher=The Citadel Press|location=New York|page=65|passage=There was a legend that Cleopatra was accustomed to '''dissolve''' pearls in vinegar and drink the fluid in order to promote her amorousness.}}
# {{lb|en|physical chemistry|intransitive}} To be [[disintegrated]] by such immersion.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[disperse]], [[drive]] [[apart]] a group of persons.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|V|v|passage=Nothing can '''dissolve''' us.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To break the continuity of; to [[disconnect]]; to [[loosen]]; to [[undo]]; to [[separate]].
#* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne
|passage=Down fell the duke, his joints '''dissolved''' asunder.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1776|title=w:The Declaration of Independence
|passage=For one people to '''dissolve''' the political bands which have connected them with another.}}
# {{lb|en|law|transitive}} To [[annul]]; to [[rescind]]; to discharge or release.
#: {{ux|en|to '''dissolve''' an injunction}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=August 13, 2017|author=Brandon Nowalk|title=Oldtown offers one last game-changing secret as Game Of Thrones goes behind enemy lines (newbies)|work=The Onion AV Club|url=http://www.avclub.com/tvclub/oldtown-offers-one-last-game-changing-secret-game--259407|passage=Jon Snow is the legitimate heir to the Targaryen line, by the old rules, of the old government, which was '''dissolved''' and reshaped by King Robert.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170817060914/http://www.avclub.com/tvclub/oldtown-offers-one-last-game-changing-secret-game--259407|archivedate=17 August 2017}}
# {{lb|en|cinematography|intransitive}} To [[shift]] from one [[shot]] to another by having the former [[fade out]] as the latter fades in.
#: {{syn|en|fade out}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[resolve]] itself as by dissolution.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[solve]]; to [[clear up]]; to [[resolve]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|poem=A Dream of Fair Women|passage='''dissolved''' the mystery}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Daniel|5|16|passage=Make interpretations and '''dissolve''' doubts.}}
# To relax by pleasure; to make powerless.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Innocence|chapter=Apology|page=29|passage=''Seraph'' and ''Cherub'', careleſs of their charge, / And wanton, in full eaſe now live at large: / Unguarded leave the paſſes of the Sky; / And all '''diſſolv'd''' in Hallelujahs lye.}}
# {{lb|en|typography}} To separate a [[ligature]] into its component [[letter]]s.

