===Etymology===
From {{der|en|enm|distaf||distaff}}, from {{der|en|ang|distæf||distaff}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=distaf|pos=n|id=MED12118|accessdate=25 December 2017}}</ref> from {{m|ang|*dis-||bunch of flax}} (cognate with {{cog|gml|dise||bunch of flax on a distaff}}) + {{m|ang|stæf||staff}} (from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stabaz||staff, stick}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*stebʰ-}}). Senses 3 and 5 (“anything traditionally done by or considered of [[importance]] to [[woman|women]] only”; “a woman, or women considered as a group”) refer to the fact that [[spinning#Noun|spinning]] was traditionally done by women.<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪstɑːf/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-distaff.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪstæf/|a=GA}}
* {{hyphenation|en|di|staff}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|distaves}}

# A [[device]] to which a [[bundle#Noun|bundle]] of [[natural#Adjective|natural]] [[fibre]]s (often [[wool]], [[flax]], or [[cotton#Noun|cotton]]) is [[attach]]ed for [[temporary]] [[storage]], before being drawn off gradually to [[spin#Verb|spin]] [[thread#Noun|thread]]. A traditional distaff is a [[staff#Noun|staff]] with flax fibres tied loosely to it (as indicated by the etymology of the word), but modern distaffs are often made of [[cord#Noun|cords]] [[weight#Verb|weighted]] with [[bead#Noun|beads]], and attached to the [[wrist]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=I|scene=iii|page=256|passage=Then hadſt thou had an excellent head of haire. {{...}} Excellent, it hangs like flax on a '''diſtaffe''': & I hope to ſee a huſwife take thee between her legs, & ſpin it off.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear Q1|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=I muſt change armes at home, and giue the '''diſtaffe''' <br> Into my Husbands hands, {{...}}}}
