# {{lb|en|linear algebra}} A [[linear sum]] in which the [[intersection]] of the summands has [[dimension]] zero. Equivalently, a linear sum of two subspaces, any vector of which can be expressed uniquely as a sum of two vectors: one vector belonging to the first subspace, and the other vector belonging to the second subspace.
# {{lb|en|linear algebra}} A [[block diagonal matrix]] interpreted as having its non-zero blocks (which are [[square matrix|square matrices]]) as summands.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Garrett Birkhoff|coauthors=Saunders Mac Lane|title=A Survey Of Modern Algebra|edition=Revised|location=U.S.A.|publisher=The Macmillan Company|year=&copy;1953|year_published=1960 (9th printing)|page=326|section=&sect;X.8}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|author=David Steven Dummit|coauthors=Richard M. Foote|title=Abstract Algebra|edition=third|location=U.S.A.|publisher=John Wiley & Sons, Inc.|year=&copy;2004|page=475|section=&sect;12.2|isbn=0-471-43334-9|oclc=758130105}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|author=Kenneth Myron Hoffman|coauthors=Ray Alden Kunze|title=Linear Algebra|edition=second|location=Upper Saddle River, New Jersey|publisher=Prentice-Hall, Inc.|year=&copy;1971|page=214|section=&sect;6.7|isbn=0-13-536797-2|oclc=1131424001}}</ref>

